    "evidence_text": "Candida albicans, a distinctive fungal model for cellular aging study\n\nTaking the advantage of morphologic changes, we are able to obtain cells of different ages.\n\nOld Candida cells tend to accumulate glycogen and oxidatively damaged proteins.\n\nThe Hallmarks of Aging\n\nUnderstanding the Odd Science of Aging\n\nHuman iPSC-based modeling of late-onset disease via progerin-induced aging.\n\nPerceived age as clinically useful biomarker of ageing: cohort study.\n\nSETTING Population based twin cohort in Denmark.   \n\n\nTwin analyses suggested that common genetic factors influence both perceived age and survival.\n\nReplicative aging in yeast: the means to the end.\n\np16INK4A therefore is a biomarker for human aging in vivo.\n\nThe data reported here suggest a model for changes in regulatory gene expression that drive aging in human skin.\n\nThe Aging Epigenome.\n\nExactly when and how this decline occurs is unknown.\n\nLamin A-dependent nuclear defects in human aging.\n\nWhether lamin A plays any role in normal aging is unknown.\n\nWe show that the same molecular mechanism responsible for HGPS is active in healthy cells.\n\nInhibition of this splice site reverses the nuclear defects associated with aging.\n\nThese observations implicate lamin A in physiological aging.",
